# 2. Environment Dynamics & Complete Constant Formalization

The HEIST environment models a high-security facility infiltration. It requires coordinated role execution.

## 2.1 Role Specialization & Action Space

The agent set $ \mathcal{N} = \{\text{scout}, \text{hacker}, \text{muscle}, \text{extractor}\} $ shares a discrete action space $ |\mathcal{A}_i| = 7 $:

$ \mathcal{A}_i = \{0: \text{UP}, 1: \text{DOWN}, 2: \text{LEFT}, 3: \text{RIGHT}, 4: \text{WAIT}, 5: \text{INTERACT}, 6: \text{BREACH}\} $

The semantics of Action 5 ($ \text{INTERACT} $) depend on the role identity $ i $. The Scout broadcasts a point of interest to allies and reveals an unoriented $ 17 \times 17 $ square bounding box. The Hacker executes multi-turn hacking on adjacent security terminals or bypasses locked doors. The Muscle temporarily knocks out a nearby guard within Manhattan distance 1. The Extractor secures loot at the vault and activates the extraction countdown.

## 2.2 Global State & Observation Encoding

Each non-scout agent receives a $ 7 \times 7 $ local grid view window. This window centers on its position and flattens to $ 49 $ scalar cell values. It concatenates with a $ 4 $-element one-hot role vector $ e_{\text{role}_i} \in \{0, 1\}^4 $. We define the local observation tensor $ o_i \in \mathbb{R}^{53} $:

$ o_i = \text{Concat}(\text{Flatten}(\text{Crop}_{7 \times 7}(\text{Grid})), e_{\text{role}_i}) \in \mathbb{R}^{53} $

Centralized critics consume the unmasked global grid layout. They read the full agent position coordinates $ (r_i, c_i)_{i=1}^4 $, guard trajectory states, and the global alarm meter $ A_t \in [0, 100] $. This forms the centralized state tensor $ s \in \mathbb{R}^{D} $.

## 2.3 Comprehensive Parameter Specification Table

Every hyperparameter and environment constant is defined as follows:

| Parameter Constant | Symbol | Value | Physical & Game-Theoretic Definition |
| :--- | :--- | :---: | :--- |
| $ \text{MAP\_SIZE} $ | $ (W, H) $ | $ (50, 50) $ | Maximum grid dimensions of benchmark facility |
| $ \text{OBSERVATION\_SIZE} $ | $ (w_{\text{obs}}, h_{\text{obs}}) $ | $ (7, 7) $ | Agent local observation view window |
| $ \text{SCOUT\_VISION\_RADIUS} $ | $ R_{\text{scout}} $ | $ 8 $ | Fog-of-war reveal radius for Scout ($ 17 \times 17 $ tile window) |
| $ \text{AGENT\_VISION\_RADIUS} $ | $ R_{\text{agent}} $ | $ 3 $ | Fog-of-war reveal radius for non-scout agents ($ 7 \times 7 $ window) |
| $ \text{HACK\_TURNS} $ | $ K_{\text{hack}} $ | $ 3 $ | Consecutive turns required at terminal to disable security |
| $ \text{EXTRACTION\_COUNTDOWN} $ | $ K_{\text{extract}} $ | $ 60 $ | Step budget remaining after extraction is called |
| $ \text{REWARD\_WIN} $ | $ R_{\text{win}} $ | $ +15.0 $ | Shared terminal return awarded upon full team extraction |
| $ \text{REWARD\_LOSE} $ | $ R_{\text{lose}} $ | $ -10.0 $ | Shared terminal return awarded on caught/alarm/timeout loss |
| $ \text{REWARD\_TASK} $ | $ R_{\text{task}} $ | $ +2.0 $ | Immediate reward for sub-goal completion |
| $ \text{REWARD\_TAG} $ | $ R_{\text{tag}} $ | $ +1.0 $ | Scout shaping reward for discovering security POIs |
| $ \text{REWARD\_TIME\_BLEED} $ | $ R_{\text{step}} $ | $ -0.01 $ | Step penalty enforcing minimum-time path execution |
| $ \text{CONVERGE\_BONUS} $ | $ C_{\text{bonus}} $ | $ +1.0 $ | Potential-based shaping coefficient for extraction exit pathing |
| $ \text{WIN\_CONVERGE\_RADIUS} $ | $ R_{\text{win\_zone}} $ | $ 3 $ | Manhattan distance to extract tile required for team win |
| $ \text{ALARM\_MAX} $ | $ A_{\text{max}} $ | $ 100.0 $ | Global alarm threshold triggering immediate episode termination |
| $ \text{ALARM\_CAMERA} $ | $ \delta A_{\text{cam}} $ | $ 0.20 $ | Alarm rate per step per agent standing in camera vision cone |
| $ \text{ALARM\_HACK\_TURN} $ | $ \delta A_{\text{hack}} $ | $ 2.00 $ | Alarm cost per turn of active terminal hacking |
| $ \text{ALARM\_BYPASS} $ | $ \delta A_{\text{door}} $ | $ 6.00 $ | Alarm penalty for forced door bypass |
| $ \text{ALARM\_NEUTRALIZE} $ | $ \delta A_{\text{neut}} $ | $ 10.00 $ | Alarm penalty when Muscle knocks out a security guard |
| $ \text{ALARM\_GUARD\_SPOT} $ | $ \delta A_{\text{spot}} $ | $ 25.00 $ | Alarm penalty when guard spots an agent within catch distance |
| $ \text{ALARM\_EXTRACTION\_TIMEOUT} $ | $ \delta A_{\text{timeout}} $ | $ 25.00 $ | Alarm surge when extraction countdown expires |
| $ \text{CAMERA\_RANGE} $ | $ L_{\text{cam}} $ | $ 12 $ | Maximum line-of-sight distance for security cameras |
| $ \text{CATCH\_DISTANCE} $ | $ d_{\text{catch}} $ | $ 1 $ | Manhattan distance triggering guard catch termination |
| $ \text{CONVERGE\_ALARM} $ | $ A_{\text{hunt}} $ | $ 50.0 $ | Alarm threshold above which guards switch to active Hunt mode |
| $ \text{NEUTRALIZE\_TURNS} $ | $ K_{\text{neut}} $ | $ 8 $ | Turns a guard remains incapacitated |
| $ \text{ALARM\_NEUTRALIZE\_DELAY} $ | $ K_{\text{delay}} $ | $ 15 $ | Delay turns before security command notices a missing guard |
| $ \text{ALARM\_BREACH} $ | $ \delta A_{\text{breach}} $ | $ 30.00 $ | Instant global-alarm cost of a wall breach |
| $ \text{BREACH\_RADIUS} $ | $ R_{\text{breach}} $ | $ 10 $ | Guards within this range repath to the breach |
| $ \text{EXTRACTOR\_BURDEN\_TURNS} $ | $ K_{\text{burden}} $ | $ 1 $ | Loot-carrying extractor moves 1 tile per $ K $ turns |
| $ \text{GUARD\_LOS\_RANGE} $ | $ L_{\text{guard}} $ | $ 8 $ | Directional line-of-sight reach for guards |
| $ \text{SEARCH\_RADIUS} $ | $ R_{\text{search}} $ | $ 5 $ | Tiles around a last-known position to sweep |
| $ \text{SEARCH\_TURNS} $ | $ K_{\text{search}} $ | $ 6 $ | Turns a guard stays in Search before Patrol |
| $ \text{BREACH\_SEARCH\_TRIGGER} $ | $ b_{\text{trigger}} $ | $ \text{True} $ | A fresh breach puts nearby guards into Search |
| $ \text{GUARD\_COUNT} $ | $ N_{\text{guard}} $ | $ 6 $ | Number of rule-based adversary guards |
| $ \text{CAMERA\_COUNT} $ | $ N_{\text{cam}} $ | $ 3 $ | Number of static security cameras |
| $ \text{DOOR\_COUNT} $ | $ N_{\text{door}} $ | $ 4 $ | Number of locked bypassable doors |

## 2.4 Potential-Based Reward Shaping (PBRS)

HEIST incorporates Potential-Based Reward Shaping to accelerate convergence in the final extraction phase.

$ \Phi(s) = - \text{Manhattan}(\text{pos}_i, \text{pos}_{\text{extract}}) $

$ F(s, s') = \gamma \Phi(s') - \Phi(s) = C_{\text{bonus}} \cdot (d_{\text{prev}} - d_{\text{cur}}) $

The term $ F(s, s') $ is a difference of potential functions $ \Phi $. Policy invariance is mathematically guaranteed.
